WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:18.269
OK, why don't we get started.

00:00:18.269 --> 00:00:20.090
So good morning everyone.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:24.320
I'm Alex Bui director of the BD2K center's
coordination center.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.349
So today we're continuing our computing overview,
with our third talk in this section.

00:00:29.349 --> 00:00:33.390
I have the pleasure of introducing Dr. Trey
Ideker from UC San Diego.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:38.760
He is a professor of genetics and bioengineering
in the Department of Medicine, and is a director

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:43.329
of the well-known National Resource for Network
Biology and the San Diego Center for Systems

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:44.329
Biology.

00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:49.149
He is a fellow at the American Association
for the Advancement of Science, as well as

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:55.239
of the American Institute for Medical and
Biological Engineering.

00:00:55.239 --> 00:00:58.399
Some of you may already actually be familiar
with Dr. Ideker's work.

00:00:58.399 --> 00:01:02.760
He is the founder of Cytoscape an opensource
bioinformatic software platform.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:08.630
That some of you may have been used before
for visualizing molecular [? interaction ?] networks.

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:13.580
So today Dr. Ideker is going to share some
of his experiences and insights into the practical

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:18.550
challenges of running a research lab at the
intersections of data science and biomedicine,

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:22.050
something that several of you will likely
face in the future.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:26.640
So Dr. Ideker, thank you for joining us today,
and the floor is yours.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.640
OK, very good.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:32.040
Thanks very much for that introduction.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:38.030
What I'm going to talk about today is really
four points that I consider to be key considerations

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:40.720
when you build a data sciences laboratory.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:46.260
I'll talk a bit about my own work through
vignettes to illustrate these four points.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:50.420
These, of course, aren't the only four points
that one might want to consider as one builds

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:56.500
out their own data sciences laboratory or
improves an existing data sciences laboratory.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:01.640
But they're certainly four of the more important
points in my experience.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:07.069
The first thing I'll talk about is lab mission,
and this is first because it underlies it

00:02:07.069 --> 00:02:10.430
underscores everything that you do in the
lab.

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:12.060
Why do you get up in the morning?

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.230
Is it technology, biology, medicine, or something
else?

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:23.930
And clearly articulating the vision as specifically
as possible.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:26.000
But with as much vision as possible.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:31.910
I'll spend most of my time in the next hour,
or in slightly less, talking about getting

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:32.910
data.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:39.600
It turns out the more you think about places
and sources of data, whether they be external

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:47.040
or internal to the lab, through collaborations,
or one on ones, it's a very key issue in the

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.200
life of a scientist.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:54.080
So most of my talk actually ends up really
focusing on one sources of data.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.380
Near the end, I'd like to touch on computing
infrastructure.

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:02.990
So of course, one needs to think as a data
scientist about what is the hardware that

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:06.190
one needs to have in one's environment.

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:09.940
And then finally, I will say a few words about
tools development process.

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:17.240
Process ends up being more important than
one might first realize in terms of one's

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:22.360
research flowing into development and ultimately
tool dissemination.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.810
So that'll come at the very end of the talk.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:29.060
So without further ado, let me take the first
issue on.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:30.060
Lab mission.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.590
And the way I think about this is really why
one

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:37.510
as a data scientist would want to go to work
in the morning, and what keeps you getting

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:40.450
up in the morning and going to work every
day.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:46.440
Of course all of us have our reasons, but
there really are three important reasons that

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:51.550
I can think of in terms of driving your research.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:53.590
One is biological discovery.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:59.620
So is the most important thing in your life
questions or statements such as, I want to

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:04.980
reveal how biology or life is structured,
of functions, and evolves.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:11.080
Now as a data scientist, let me make it clear
that you are going to be using the tool box,

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:14.210
among other tools, of data science.

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:19.190
A chemist could ask this question, a geneticist
could ask this question and they would be

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:20.440
using a different toolbox.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:24.880
And of course one's free to use multiple tools
from those different boxes.

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:32.419
But the point is it's very possible to be
a data scientist, yet your first allegiance

00:04:32.419 --> 00:04:38.240
is to biological discovery, which might mean
you largely use state of the art tools that

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:41.900
already exist to answer those questions.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:44.270
The next question would be medicine.

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:49.719
So independent of how life actually works,
I want to help people get better.

00:04:49.719 --> 00:04:55.099
I want to cure cancer would be, if I had to
stage my lab's goal, from the medical point

00:04:55.099 --> 00:04:56.159
of view.

00:04:56.159 --> 00:05:02.080
But again, you want to cure cancer using a
data scientist toolbox and increasingly I

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:08.789
think we're seeing that the ability to cure
diseases and to push for biomedical research

00:05:08.789 --> 00:05:13.090
into those diseases is dependent on data science.

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:18.480
And finally is listed here is probably what's
the most common answer to this question of

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.729
among data science individuals or labs which
is, what gets you up in the morning is the

00:05:23.729 --> 00:05:27.300
urge to develop new technology or methodology.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:35.659
So you have, and this is commonly and unfortunately
in a derogatory way, sort of referred to as

00:05:35.659 --> 00:05:37.270
a hammer in search of a nail.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:44.880
But of course hammers can easily find the
correct nail lots of the time.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:49.159
But the point is, you've built a hammer and
you have a data science analysis, you have

00:05:49.159 --> 00:05:55.089
an algorithm, a software or hardware device,
etc. that you believe is going to revolutionize,

00:05:55.089 --> 00:06:01.319
largely, the previous two disciplines, either
biological discovery, and or medicine.

00:06:01.319 --> 00:06:06.379
So now, like I'll do throughout this talk,
I'll try to make some general points and then

00:06:06.379 --> 00:06:13.469
spend a few slides illustrating how I answer
those questions in my own life and lab.

00:06:13.469 --> 00:06:20.249
So I am driven to get up in the morning because
I have the as you'll see not an unambitious

00:06:20.249 --> 00:06:24.409
goal, I'd like to build a complete model of
cell structure and function and fully automate

00:06:24.409 --> 00:06:31.059
that process, much like one can now build
a full model of a genome through automated,

00:06:31.059 --> 00:06:36.759
or at least semi automated genome sequencing
pipelines.

00:06:36.759 --> 00:06:44.719
And so just to give you a couple of motivations
and arguments for why I think that is an important

00:06:44.719 --> 00:06:51.189
motivation in my life, I'm driven by this
idea lately that networks, like people are

00:06:51.189 --> 00:06:56.999
generating on the left here, of either physical
protein, protein interaction, or gene expression,

00:06:56.999 --> 00:06:58.610
or so-called genetic interaction.

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:04.229
There's lots of ways that scientists are linking
together molecules like genes and proteins

00:07:04.229 --> 00:07:06.860
and cells.

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:13.699
The problem is with this common view is that
cells don't look like that.

00:07:13.699 --> 00:07:18.669
Cells look much more like the picture you
see on the right, and it turns out that both

00:07:18.669 --> 00:07:23.250
the picture on the right and the left are
the same object, or representations of the

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:24.279
same object.

00:07:24.279 --> 00:07:27.389
And in cells, this is the proteasome.

00:07:27.389 --> 00:07:30.349
The proteasome, as you may know, is the vacuum
cleaner of the cell.

00:07:30.349 --> 00:07:32.899
We're looking at the proteasome on its side.

00:07:32.899 --> 00:07:39.289
So here is the core in the middle that eats
the proteins, and here is the regulatory particle

00:07:39.289 --> 00:07:43.449
on either side, and you can see of course,
the several [? faults ?] of symmetry there.

00:07:43.449 --> 00:07:47.949
It's very hard to see that structure on the
left.

00:07:47.949 --> 00:07:52.430
Although if one starts to peer at it, one
sees a higher density of interactions among

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:57.030
the core proteins, which here are the same
colors, in red and yellow.

00:07:57.030 --> 00:08:01.210
And one sees a higher density of protein and
other interactions on the right side, among

00:08:01.210 --> 00:08:03.379
the regulatory sub units.

00:08:03.379 --> 00:08:09.289
But the question that, really, we've been
addressing now towards a ultimately model

00:08:09.289 --> 00:08:12.819
of the structure and function of the cell,
which starts to look much more like the picture

00:08:12.819 --> 00:08:15.160
on the right, is as follows.

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:20.759
So we're trying to come up with algorithms
and tools from data sciences point of view

00:08:20.759 --> 00:08:25.710
that will take all of these network data,
that I just showed you here and here is in

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:30.580
panel A.
And first computing master gene-gene similarity

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:37.740
network, based on all of those raw interaction
data, but don't stop there, from gene-gene

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:43.500
similarity network ask the question, how far
can we push that towards what starts to look

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:45.140
like models of the cell?

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:50.250
And as far as we've gotten so far, we haven't,
I should admit, gone all the way toward structure.

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:52.050
That's a very, very hard problem.

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:58.089
But we have been able to push the towards
hierarchical models of the components of cells.

00:08:58.089 --> 00:09:02.370
So you saw in the proteasomal case, there
were already several orders of hierarchy the

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:05.660
proteasome contained a core and a regulatory
particle.

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:09.810
Each of those contains sub units, each of
those sub units contain proteins.

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:15.589
So we've at least gotten as far as being able
to flesh out that type of structure, largely

00:09:15.589 --> 00:09:18.470
directly from these kinds of interaction data.

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:23.990
That hierarchy begins to look a lot like what
we've already heard about in this series an

00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:24.990
ontology.

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:31.480
So we directly align those ontologies with
the gene ontology to see what is the similarity

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:33.680
with what's been curated from the literature.

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:38.290
Of course this hierarchy I'm talking about
now was entirely data driven.

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:44.709
So just quickly, the way the way that works
is, it's a clustering algorithm with a couple

00:09:44.709 --> 00:09:45.730
of key twists.

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:50.360
Rather than build a binary tree, like most
clustering algorithms, or many clustering

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:56.050
algorithms do, you want to build what's called
a directed [INAUDIBLE] graph, which is, not

00:09:56.050 --> 00:10:00.380
coincidentally, the structure of a gene ontology
or other ontology.

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:07.160
Which means that internal clusters, first
of all, can have more than two children.

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:09.990
So here's an internal cluster that has three
children.

00:10:09.990 --> 00:10:11.420
Why is that a good idea?

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:15.779
Because if you have a protein complex, it
can certainly have more than two sub units.

00:10:15.779 --> 00:10:21.050
It can have arbitrary numbers, and we also
want to allow for [? cliatropy, ?] the idea

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:25.470
that an internal cluster can participate in
two or more parent clusters.

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:27.180
So it's a varying of clustering with that
twist, and afterwards you align it to a literature

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:28.920
curated ontology.
of clustering with that twist, and afterwards

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:33.470
you align it to a literature curated ontology.

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:37.050
And then just to give you an example of how
this works, this process was just recently

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:42.100
applied by my very capable grad student, [? Mike
?] Kramer, to autophagy.

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:46.629
And so here he's generated, or I should say,
analyzed, and we'll get to this in a second,

00:10:46.629 --> 00:10:53.620
in the public domain, a bunch of network data,
like protein-protein interactions, coexpression,

00:10:53.620 --> 00:10:55.770
and other types of networks.

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:57.670
Among the autophagy genes.

00:10:57.670 --> 00:11:01.960
Autophagy genes typically start with ATG for
autophagy.

00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:06.500
Now you'll suspend your disbelief here because
we're not showing you the raw data but he's

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:11.410
already processed these into an overall similarity
score between pairs of genes.

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:17.259
So you can see in the interaction data, genes
like ATG 27 and 10 are more similar, genes

00:11:17.259 --> 00:11:20.170
like 10, 12 and 7 are more similar.

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:23.540
Ah and look, ATG 10 participates in both of
those clusters.

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:29.079
So already you need a clustering algorithm
that can correctly create both clusters for

00:11:29.079 --> 00:11:30.589
one component.

00:11:30.589 --> 00:11:35.500
And oh look, there's a much larger cluster
here which encompasses all of these genes

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:38.290
ATG 10 all the way to ATG 8.

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:43.579
So in the hierarchy, at right, you can start
to see that structure in what we hope is a

00:11:43.579 --> 00:11:49.589
much more clear and interpretable way than
looking at either the raw interact omics data

00:11:49.589 --> 00:11:50.910
or these heat maps.

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:56.680
So you can see here that ATG 10 is placed
into two higher order components defined exactly

00:11:56.680 --> 00:12:03.100
by these data, and now we have a larger component
here, which contains all of these ATG genes.

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:08.500
The names that you see on, at least, these
blue terms come from alignment to the gene

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:09.740
ontology.

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:16.601
In the case of these red, internal subsystems,
or terms, those were not in the gene ontology

00:12:16.601 --> 00:12:19.350
but it turns out, have literature supporting
them.

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:24.431
And so we were able to name those orphaned
terms and submit those names to the gene ontology

00:12:24.431 --> 00:12:26.930
and those are now in that resource.

00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:32.660
And then finally when you see a number here
like 183, this is a truly orphaned component.

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:37.389
We have evidence that it's a true component
based on the data, yet there is no evidence

00:12:37.389 --> 00:12:41.769
and [? go ?] for that particular grouping,
and there's no evidence in current literature

00:12:41.769 --> 00:12:45.860
for that particular and beyond [? go.

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:47.860
?]
You can, of course, zoom out and look not

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:54.980
just at autophagy but the entire cells worth
of genes, and so this tree here starts with

00:12:54.980 --> 00:12:59.170
all genes underneath the root, again, through
alignment to [? go.

00:12:59.170 --> 00:13:03.769
?] This is labeled a scalar component, branch
of [? go, ?] because that's what we aligned

00:13:03.769 --> 00:13:04.769
against.

00:13:04.769 --> 00:13:09.189
And then you see quickly you partition into
a mitochondrial part, a membrane part, and

00:13:09.189 --> 00:13:14.160
intracellular part, especially the intracellular
part, you can name because [? go ?] names

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:15.959
lots of these internal structures.

00:13:15.959 --> 00:13:20.880
But there's lots that's unnamed, and those
are exactly the new biological discoveries.

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:27.709
So to get back to the example of the proteasome,
here is on right here is the old picture that

00:13:27.709 --> 00:13:32.709
you would view that in a network browser,
but now if you look at it from the hierarchical

00:13:32.709 --> 00:13:37.819
or ontological point of view here, again based
on the exact same data you start to see the

00:13:37.819 --> 00:13:41.509
structure of the proteasome, at least in terms
of the hierarchy, where

00:13:41.509 --> 00:13:46.639
you have a proteasome complex, which splits
into a core and a regulatory particle, which

00:13:46.639 --> 00:13:50.420
split into subunits, and proteins, and so
forth.

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:56.529
So that's an example that I'll carry forward
throughout this talk.

00:13:56.529 --> 00:13:59.930
But let's get back to how that relates to
the lab mission.

00:13:59.930 --> 00:14:06.629
So that definitely is a new technology rather
than a biological discovery or a medical discovery.

00:14:06.629 --> 00:14:12.930
However, of course, once you are driven by
one of these goals-- which I'm clearly driven

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:19.860
by the third one here-then you have ample
opportunities, if you play your cards correctly,

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:26.779
to imagine at least how those would then bear
on biological discovering and medicine.

00:14:26.779 --> 00:14:29.249
And we'll see examples of that in a little
bit.

00:14:29.249 --> 00:14:35.980
But first, let me now talk about the second
and what ends up being the majority of this

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:42.910
talk, which is the very exciting and sometimes
thorny issue of where you get data as a data

00:14:42.910 --> 00:14:44.240
scientist.

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:52.540
Like any topic, one can try to classify it
into different parts.

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:59.110
So here what I've done is try to spread out
different ways of getting data from-- or across

00:14:59.110 --> 00:15:04.750
a continuum, where you have easy entry but
less novelty and control all the way where

00:15:04.750 --> 00:15:11.410
you have a higher bar of entry but complete
control and hopefully more more novelty.

00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:15.040
Although this, of course, is a broad generalization.

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:20.040
And there's lots of novelty certainly to be
found all throughout the spectrum.

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:26.990
So the easiest bar to entry, as a starting
data scientist, is of course public data sets.

00:15:26.990 --> 00:15:32.370
It's surprising the number of data science
labs that have a perfectly good career with

00:15:32.370 --> 00:15:37.740
only looking at public data sets, without
doing any of these other four activities that

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:39.350
I'm about to talk to you.

00:15:39.350 --> 00:15:44.689
Now, if one does not like to operate in a
vacuum-- and increasingly in our connected

00:15:44.689 --> 00:15:52.209
society we are encouraged and have ample opportunity
to work together-- one can think about accessing

00:15:52.209 --> 00:15:54.610
data through different kinds of collaborations.

00:15:54.610 --> 00:16:00.579
There's really many ways to think about this,
but I think about it in terms of two types--

00:16:00.579 --> 00:16:06.170
many-tomany collaborations which are sometimes
called consortia and one-on-one more focused

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:12.259
collaborations between you and a coworker
who can be either at your same institution

00:16:12.259 --> 00:16:15.970
or at a different institution.

00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:22.569
And then finally, pushing down this pipeline
or this continuum, the last category would

00:16:22.569 --> 00:16:26.490
be to bring those experiments into your own
lab.

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:32.870
And the way to do that is if you yourself
do not feel yet qualified, if you have not

00:16:32.870 --> 00:16:38.680
been trained in biochemistry or genetics or
some other molecular biological laboratory

00:16:38.680 --> 00:16:45.579
discipline, then I've seen lots of cases where
that lab nonetheless is able to run a very--

00:16:45.579 --> 00:16:49.110
well, to start and then run a very successful
experimental program.

00:16:49.110 --> 00:16:51.209
And the way to do it is with a host lab.

00:16:51.209 --> 00:16:55.589
So this requires a lot of trust with the host
partner here.

00:16:55.589 --> 00:17:02.209
But if you have, say, a neighbor with whom
you have a very good working relationship,

00:17:02.209 --> 00:17:06.809
then you can often come up with an agreement
where you'll pay for an experimentalist in

00:17:06.809 --> 00:17:13.630
their lab or you've already hired a postdoc
or a technician into your lab and they'll

00:17:13.630 --> 00:17:19.350
mentor that person within their own lab at
least some of the time.

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:25.450
The final position on this continuum is to
go even further and to take on all that experimental

00:17:25.450 --> 00:17:27.680
laboratory activity yourself.

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:32.760
And again, there is a lot of laboratories,
including my own, that took this plunge at

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:34.770
the beginning of their careers.

00:17:34.770 --> 00:17:37.670
And I certainly wouldn't go back at this point.

00:17:37.670 --> 00:17:40.090
And I'll talk a bit about that.

00:17:40.090 --> 00:17:45.260
But all of these modes I'd like to say are
successful ways of running a data sciences

00:17:45.260 --> 00:17:46.260
lab.

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:49.910
You just have to choose which kind of lab
you want to be.

00:17:49.910 --> 00:17:55.410
So in terms of public data, because a lot
of what I'm focused on is building models

00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:59.620
of cells with ultimate application to understanding
and curing cancer.

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:04.380
And again, every time I say that, I have to
smile, and I'm sure some of you are as well.

00:18:04.380 --> 00:18:07.100
It's a lofty goal here.

00:18:07.100 --> 00:18:12.660
So-- but-- but-- but the point is you want
to keep getting out of bed for 30 years, or

00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:13.660
maybe your entire career.

00:18:13.660 --> 00:18:18.270
You don't want to just have a goal that's
going to get you out of bed this week.

00:18:18.270 --> 00:18:26.600
So in the case of cancer, there is-- in many
ways, cancer is the flag ship of widely available

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:27.600
public data.

00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:36.030
And the jewel in the eye of that flagship
is the cancer genome atlas, or TCGA.

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:42.610
Where at this point in time, there there is
far in excess of 10,000 exomes, I should say

00:18:42.610 --> 00:18:46.310
here as opposed to genomes, for cancer available.

00:18:46.310 --> 00:18:52.950
This just shows one such genome that was an
early genome generated by the cancer genome

00:18:52.950 --> 00:18:53.950
atlas.

00:18:53.950 --> 00:19:01.020
This is a so-called Circos plot here, for
a single patient who had been sequenced, and

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:05.980
also subjected to a variety of other modes
of data collection and analysis.

00:19:05.980 --> 00:19:07.920
But here they're really focusing on the genome.

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:14.820
So you can see here around the circle, you
have the 22 chromosomes plus the x and the

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:15.820
y.

00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:22.950
And then in successively smaller and smaller
tracks around this circle, you can see different

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:28.220
types of analysis they've performed to extract
different kinds of information about the raw

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:29.740
genomic sequence.

00:19:29.740 --> 00:19:35.610
So in terms of single nucleotide substitutions,
you're looking at that here in this yellow

00:19:35.610 --> 00:19:41.840
track, just counting the number you see as
you walk along the chromosomes from one to

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:42.900
the other.

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:50.520
You can then get into the different kinds
of substitutions you see there and other kinds

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:51.690
of changes.

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:55.520
You can then get at the copy number variance
or long segments of DNA that are amplified

00:19:55.520 --> 00:19:56.520
or deleted.

00:19:56.520 --> 00:20:02.110
So here you can look at amplifications, deletions,
and so on.

00:20:02.110 --> 00:20:09.090
And so then if you take a step back and look
at the collection of all data that's available

00:20:09.090 --> 00:20:14.030
through a resource like the cancer genome
atlas, they've really made it very, very accessible.

00:20:14.030 --> 00:20:23.030
So a few clicks away, if you simply Google
TCGA cancer, you can get to specific tissues

00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:27.180
where all of the patient data that have been
collected for that specific cancer tissue

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:29.190
can be accessed.

00:20:29.190 --> 00:20:34.210
So if one is interested in head and neck squamous
cell carcinoma, here's the data page that

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:35.680
you find.

00:20:35.680 --> 00:20:43.540
So there's a total of 528 cases currently
in the TCGA, of which just shy of 500 have

00:20:43.540 --> 00:20:45.630
been sequenced, at least for the exome.

00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:50.100
But then I mentioned, they've collected a
whole variety of layers of molecular and then

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:53.590
finally clinical information, which are mostly
survival.

00:20:53.590 --> 00:20:58.020
Occasionally you get direct response information
in the clinical column here.

00:20:58.020 --> 00:21:03.650
But it's a lot of data and at least the first
couple of tiers of those data are pretty easily

00:21:03.650 --> 00:21:04.650
accessible.

00:21:04.650 --> 00:21:10.340
If one wants increasingly raw data, one has
to obtain the proper access but hundreds,

00:21:10.340 --> 00:21:14.290
if not thousands, of labs have taken that
step, including my own.

00:21:14.290 --> 00:21:20.580
So it's not complicated to do.

00:21:20.580 --> 00:21:26.920
Now my lab being a network lab, we also are
very interested in public domain data sets

00:21:26.920 --> 00:21:32.320
describing molecular networks, of which--
just like cancer genomes-- there are many.

00:21:32.320 --> 00:21:37.530
Here I just list general categories without
mentioning the individual databases.

00:21:37.530 --> 00:21:40.710
I'll talk about that maybe a little bit later.

00:21:40.710 --> 00:21:48.840
But you have metabolic networks that are essentially
akin to the whole Boehringer Mannheim wall

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:53.940
charts people used to have in the labs-- on
the lab wall.

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:57.760
Transcriptional networks which have been measured
increasingly systematically with techniques

00:21:57.760 --> 00:21:59.690
like chIP seq.

00:21:59.690 --> 00:22:03.860
Genetic and protein interaction networks--
protein interaction networks are being measured

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:10.140
with technologies like [? authenity ?] [? purification
?] [INAUDIBLE] or the [INAUDIBLE] hybrid system

00:22:10.140 --> 00:22:14.090
and a variety of other related experimental
technologies.

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:19.610
And again, many labs are generating these
data and depositing them in the public domain.

00:22:19.610 --> 00:22:24.950
If all else fails, one can always take the
prolific MNRA and protein expression data

00:22:24.950 --> 00:22:29.930
that are out there in databases like the Gene
Expression Omnibus, or GL.

00:22:29.930 --> 00:22:35.690
And from those profiles of MRNA levels for
each gene, compute genegene similarities which

00:22:35.690 --> 00:22:39.130
can be interpreted and used as networks.

00:22:39.130 --> 00:22:45.750
We started putting, in my own lab, a lot of
these data but more specifically, not just

00:22:45.750 --> 00:22:50.690
the raw interaction data, but some of the
subnetworks in the cell models derived from

00:22:50.690 --> 00:22:57.300
those data into this database called the Network
Data Exchange, or NDEx.

00:22:57.300 --> 00:23:04.620
This now contains-- here we show just a variety
of sources of networks this thing contains.

00:23:04.620 --> 00:23:10.650
We certainly have a focus on cancer networks
like the NCI pathways interaction database

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:12.730
we're the host of here.

00:23:12.730 --> 00:23:16.520
Because we were funded by the NCI and our
core interest is cancer.

00:23:16.520 --> 00:23:24.500
But there is a variety of diseases and pathways
related to those here in the database.

00:23:24.500 --> 00:23:33.260
Now when you have various kinds of data in
the public domain, I just want to give you

00:23:33.260 --> 00:23:38.270
another vignette of ways that our lab in a
purely public domain, data-driven way has

00:23:38.270 --> 00:23:44.540
been able to put together these different
data sets for productive line of research.

00:23:44.540 --> 00:23:52.550
And so, what we thought we could do is use
these protein networks as a scaffold of potential

00:23:52.550 --> 00:24:02.010
pathways in which or on which to integrate
heterogeneous patient cancer mutation profiles

00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:05.580
and heterogeneous patient MRNA expression
levels.

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:11.270
Today I'll focus mainly on the patient cancer
mutation profiles, with the idea here, can

00:24:11.270 --> 00:24:18.220
you use the networks to group patients that
may have different genes mutated into tumors,

00:24:18.220 --> 00:24:25.100
but nonetheless are mutating the same networks
and pathways as a way of taking what have

00:24:25.100 --> 00:24:27.120
been called in [INAUDIBLE] patients-- those
that can't be

00:24:27.120 --> 00:24:33.720
grouped with any others for diagnosis or therapy--
and getting extra power and insight for grouping

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:36.220
them at the pathway level.

00:24:36.220 --> 00:24:43.050
The algorithm we used here to do this integration
is a algorithm that's been very powerful in

00:24:43.050 --> 00:24:46.030
network science as a whole.

00:24:46.030 --> 00:24:47.300
It's called network propagation.

00:24:47.300 --> 00:24:53.220
It also goes by the name of network smoothing
or sometimes diffusion, sometimes random walk

00:24:53.220 --> 00:24:54.540
models are invoked.

00:24:54.540 --> 00:25:00.990
All of these names are essentially synonymous
for the same underlying method.

00:25:00.990 --> 00:25:06.020
And I won't go into the mathematics here,
but simply take you through a high level intuition

00:25:06.020 --> 00:25:09.850
of how this approach works.

00:25:09.850 --> 00:25:12.620
I should say these-- I'm citing two papers
here.

00:25:12.620 --> 00:25:18.760
One is [INAUDIBLE] lab from Tel Aviv, [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:18.760 --> 00:25:24.780
And the other is from Ben Raphael's lab, who
used this technique to develop a very successful

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:26.960
approach called HotNet.

00:25:26.960 --> 00:25:31.010
And then I'll talk about in the next slides,
some of our activities.

00:25:31.010 --> 00:25:37.030
But the idea here of network propagation is
that if you have a patient mutation data set,

00:25:37.030 --> 00:25:43.531
I'm calling the patient genome type 1 in yellow
or patient genotype 2 in blue, the data set

00:25:43.531 --> 00:25:47.750
will indicate some mutations in genes for
that patient.

00:25:47.750 --> 00:25:52.620
So here are the yellow mutations in the yellow
patient, and the blue mutations in the blue

00:25:52.620 --> 00:25:53.620
patient.

00:25:53.620 --> 00:25:56.950
These data are extremely heterogeneous, motivating
the need for this approach.

00:25:56.950 --> 00:26:01.780
But occasionally, you do find patients will
mutate the same commonly mutated cancer gene.

00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:06.730
Maybe this green gene here that's mutating
in both patients is a very common player in

00:26:06.730 --> 00:26:09.710
cancer like p53.

00:26:09.710 --> 00:26:13.152
But the question is, can you find more commonality
than that at the network level.

00:26:13.152 --> 00:26:18.670
And so the way propagation works is it pretends
that each mutation is a source of heat.

00:26:18.670 --> 00:26:23.040
And that the protein interactions and other
molecular interactions you have in this network

00:26:23.040 --> 00:26:29.280
can conduct that heat to their network neighbors,
and their neighbors' neighbors and so on,

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:31.210
to convergence.

00:26:31.210 --> 00:26:34.540
And convergence depends on one parameter.

00:26:34.540 --> 00:26:39.300
It depends on how much-- it's essentially
the ratio of heat you inject to the amount

00:26:39.300 --> 00:26:43.580
of heat at every unit time being diffused
into the atmosphere.

00:26:43.580 --> 00:26:48.100
So you can think of that heat evaporating
or diffusing upwards from this network into

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:49.420
the atmosphere.

00:26:49.420 --> 00:26:56.360
And so, at steady state, you get some spreading
from the heat sources with those being the

00:26:56.360 --> 00:26:58.900
hottest, and their neighbors being the hottest.

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:03.210
And then the temperature falling off around
that, depending on the setting [? of its ?] parameter.

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:08.090
And so having diffused those two data sets,
you now see that there is a large green region

00:27:08.090 --> 00:27:14.140
over here, which represents hot proteins in
both patients.

00:27:14.140 --> 00:27:21.210
That is to say, what we've done is we've rescored
a patient profile from a few 1's in a sea

00:27:21.210 --> 00:27:26.670
of 0's for the genes that aren't mutated to
a quantitative score for every gene indicating

00:27:26.670 --> 00:27:32.720
not whether it has mutated in a patient, but
its network proximity to mutations.

00:27:32.720 --> 00:27:34.400
So if you have an upstream factor, it feels
the heat.

00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:37.940
If you have a downstream factor, it feels
the heat and some

00:27:37.940 --> 00:27:39.470
kind of signaling pathway.

00:27:39.470 --> 00:27:44.680
If you have a member of the same protein complex,
then it feels the heat as the other member

00:27:44.680 --> 00:27:45.680
that was mutated.

00:27:45.680 --> 00:27:49.720
And that's how you can begin to get the striking
now similarities between patients.

00:27:49.720 --> 00:27:55.140
Again Ben Raphael, using a HotNet algorithm
has been very successful at finding cancer

00:27:55.140 --> 00:27:57.440
pathways using this approach.

00:27:57.440 --> 00:28:03.010
And in our hands, we pivoted the question
and tried to stratify patients using this

00:28:03.010 --> 00:28:04.010
approach.

00:28:04.010 --> 00:28:10.590
And so here is a network that stratifies and
identifies a particularly aggressive subtype

00:28:10.590 --> 00:28:13.200
of ovarian cancer.

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:21.840
The idea here is that while hardly any two
patients in this subtype mutate the same gene,

00:28:21.840 --> 00:28:28.650
all of the patients in this subtype mutated
gene somewhere on this slide in this subnetwork.

00:28:28.650 --> 00:28:30.960
And that's how the network knowledge helps.

00:28:30.960 --> 00:28:36.820
Here I'm showing you evidence that these subtypes
are significant at least clinically or with

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:43.000
respect to survival this blue subtype is that
aggressive network I just showed you.

00:28:43.000 --> 00:28:49.030
So if you're mutated in that, then you are
in the blue subtype with poor survival.

00:28:49.030 --> 00:28:55.220
That poor survival replicates in future cohorts
that have come out after our initial study.

00:28:55.220 --> 00:29:01.230
This is a second large public repository of
cancer data, by the way, the International

00:29:01.230 --> 00:29:03.200
Cancer Genomics Consortium.

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:08.060
This is an Australian cohort of ovarian--
or of women with ovarian cancer.

00:29:08.060 --> 00:29:12.940
And here we're essentially not re-formulating
this diagnostic, we're just using it to show

00:29:12.940 --> 00:29:17.830
that if you mutate that same network, you
also get poor survival outcomes.

00:29:17.830 --> 00:29:19.180
OK.

00:29:19.180 --> 00:29:24.140
So that's one vignette from my group, where
it's really-- we're actually now seeing two

00:29:24.140 --> 00:29:29.930
vignettes, I should say, where really we're
focused entirely on public data sets.

00:29:29.930 --> 00:29:36.130
And if you can argue TCGA was actually the
result of a large collaboration.

00:29:36.130 --> 00:29:40.330
Although what's interesting is my group is
not-- or at least was not at the beginning--

00:29:40.330 --> 00:29:41.780
part of TCGA.

00:29:41.780 --> 00:29:47.270
We became involved near the end, only after
the success with this method on the public

00:29:47.270 --> 00:29:50.340
data they made available.

00:29:50.340 --> 00:29:55.240
If you're interested in joining a consortium
from the get go, with respect to cancer even,

00:29:55.240 --> 00:30:02.050
there are-- here I've made a short list of
four different consortia that either are just

00:30:02.050 --> 00:30:04.210
completing or just getting started.

00:30:04.210 --> 00:30:07.330
So the Cancer Genome Atlas itself has officially
ended.

00:30:07.330 --> 00:30:11.840
But that is already giving rise to what's
been called the pan-cancer analysis of whole

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:12.840
genomes.

00:30:12.840 --> 00:30:18.160
And that's well underway, transitioning from
exome analysis to pan-cancer or full genome

00:30:18.160 --> 00:30:24.110
analysis of some of the same cancer tumors.

00:30:24.110 --> 00:30:30.130
There's other satellite projects, such as
the Pre-cancer Genome Atlas, the Cancer Immunity

00:30:30.130 --> 00:30:31.130
Atlas.

00:30:31.130 --> 00:30:35.970
I'll talk in just a slide about the Cancer
Cell Map Initiative that I've been involved

00:30:35.970 --> 00:30:37.440
in formation of.

00:30:37.440 --> 00:30:44.640
There's a Cancer Cell Atlas, which seeks to
define all cell types at the single-cell RNA

00:30:44.640 --> 00:30:46.320
sequencing level and cancer.

00:30:46.320 --> 00:30:51.420
This project is just getting underway and
it's also a consortium, and so on and so forth.

00:30:51.420 --> 00:30:57.650
Cell Explorer, for instance, involves imaging
and advanced image recognition capability

00:30:57.650 --> 00:31:02.550
and building databases of tumor cell images.

00:31:02.550 --> 00:31:04.940
So again, this is just cancer.

00:31:04.940 --> 00:31:09.060
There is a short list in any disease-focused
area now of large

00:31:09.060 --> 00:31:13.220
consortia to become involved in if you so
choose.

00:31:13.220 --> 00:31:19.480
And depending on this stage of your career,
I think it's either advisable or inadvisable.

00:31:19.480 --> 00:31:26.920
I think the clear pro of a consortium like
one of these is that there is enormous energy

00:31:26.920 --> 00:31:32.170
in the critical mass of folks, and also funding
that can be had in a critical mass of folks

00:31:32.170 --> 00:31:36.560
that come together around a common cause.

00:31:36.560 --> 00:31:40.420
The con is that one can become lost in all
of that.

00:31:40.420 --> 00:31:49.050
And so if I had any advice to someone starting
their data sciences laboratory, it would be

00:31:49.050 --> 00:31:54.100
certainly be involved and don't make it the
only thing that you do.

00:31:54.100 --> 00:31:59.600
I think in this day and age it's still worth
having your independent research laboratory

00:31:59.600 --> 00:32:08.740
activity and pursuing these consortiums or
these consortia as a secondary activity.

00:32:08.740 --> 00:32:15.360
But the pros are really quite strong, I must
admit.

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:19.620
And so here's-- here's just the one we've
recently formed around networks with the goal

00:32:19.620 --> 00:32:26.950
to-- having now sequenced many, many different
cancer genomes, can we now understand the

00:32:26.950 --> 00:32:32.870
higher order proteins network structures that
help us explain some of those mutations, and

00:32:32.870 --> 00:32:39.170
marshalling people together to try to systematically
define those protein-protein in genetic interaction

00:32:39.170 --> 00:32:42.760
and transcriptional interaction networks.

00:32:42.760 --> 00:32:50.560
So now when we talk about one-on-one collaborations,
one of the nice things about having started

00:32:50.560 --> 00:32:56.330
with public data and showing proof of concept
of a particular approach with a tool that

00:32:56.330 --> 00:32:59.790
might come out of your lab, is now people
might get interested.

00:32:59.790 --> 00:33:03.180
And of course, they can get interested by
reading your papers.

00:33:03.180 --> 00:33:09.070
They can also get interested by you reaching
out to them, depending on whether they're

00:33:09.070 --> 00:33:12.780
at your same institution or they're at a different
institution.

00:33:12.780 --> 00:33:15.260
That's either easier or harder.

00:33:15.260 --> 00:33:20.060
But certainly, conferences are a great place
to form those connections even across institutions.

00:33:20.060 --> 00:33:27.200
This is a collaboration that I became involved
with after that network stratification paper

00:33:27.200 --> 00:33:28.200
we published in 2013.

00:33:28.200 --> 00:33:33.180
One of my colleagues here at UC San Diego
is Joe Gleason.

00:33:33.180 --> 00:33:34.980
He was not studying cancer.

00:33:34.980 --> 00:33:37.250
He studies rare childhood diseases.

00:33:37.250 --> 00:33:42.220
In this particular case, he was studying a
disease called Hereditary Spastic Paraplegia

00:33:42.220 --> 00:33:43.220
or HSP.

00:33:43.220 --> 00:33:49.780
Joe tells me that HSP is what they call a
complex Mendelian disorder, which means that

00:33:49.780 --> 00:33:56.260
in any one family or child, it's one gene
typically that causes that disease.

00:33:56.260 --> 00:34:01.300
But from pedigree to pedigree of unrelated
families, then it's typically found that it's

00:34:01.300 --> 00:34:02.300
different genes.

00:34:02.300 --> 00:34:03.980
And that's the complexity.

00:34:03.980 --> 00:34:10.409
So here, we were able to work together using
essentially-- not the exact same but what's

00:34:10.409 --> 00:34:14.450
essentially a very similar approach to the
one I just described with network propagation

00:34:14.450 --> 00:34:24.840
here-- to find a number of disease genes for
this HSP disease based on their proximity

00:34:24.840 --> 00:34:27.690
through networks to known disease traits.

00:34:27.690 --> 00:34:33.250
And so what you're seeing here are the seed
or known proteins from before this study.

00:34:33.250 --> 00:34:39.399
And red are the candidate genes where we have
power for some of these genes to find them

00:34:39.399 --> 00:34:41.899
only because of the network proximity that
you

00:34:41.899 --> 00:34:42.899
see here.

00:34:42.899 --> 00:34:49.710
If you just looked at the marginal association
of mutations or variants in these genes with

00:34:49.710 --> 00:34:54.770
the phenotype, without network knowledge knitting
genes together, you would not [? be ?] [? power

00:34:54.770 --> 00:34:57.240
?] to find those genes.

00:34:57.240 --> 00:35:05.790
So that's an example of playing between collaboration
and one's own work with public data set activity.

00:35:05.790 --> 00:35:12.980
But now let me talk about the last two categories
here, which are transitioning to get these

00:35:12.980 --> 00:35:18.140
kinds of experiments going in your own lab.

00:35:18.140 --> 00:35:26.580
And for this point, I want to invoke my hero
Sir [? Ari ?] [? Fisher ?] who has two very

00:35:26.580 --> 00:35:28.060
relevant quotes.

00:35:28.060 --> 00:35:33.820
I don't actually know if [? Ari ?] [? Fisher
?] had experiments going in his lab.

00:35:33.820 --> 00:35:35.910
I think he did a lot of collaboration.

00:35:35.910 --> 00:35:43.260
But he had a very famous quote, and then a
not-so-well-known quote that's relevant.

00:35:43.260 --> 00:35:50.080
The first point is that if you hold your experiments
close, you control the experimental design

00:35:50.080 --> 00:35:52.460
and validation.

00:35:52.460 --> 00:35:56.650
And so the famous quote is to consult the
statistician after the experiment is finished

00:35:56.650 --> 00:35:59.870
is often literally to ask him to conduct a
post-mortem examination.

00:35:59.870 --> 00:36:04.500
He can perhaps say what the experiment died
of.

00:36:04.500 --> 00:36:10.730
And so therein lies the problem that I think
data scientists experience all the time.

00:36:10.730 --> 00:36:17.220
This one-on-one collaborator comes to you
and it's essentially all the work in lab has

00:36:17.220 --> 00:36:18.270
been done.

00:36:18.270 --> 00:36:23.470
And you can immediately see five things you
would have suggested they change about the

00:36:23.470 --> 00:36:24.470
experiment.

00:36:24.470 --> 00:36:29.320
And of course, if you had started at the beginning
of the projects, you might have learned of

00:36:29.320 --> 00:36:34.660
course as much as they would have about the
particular biological problem at hand, causing

00:36:34.660 --> 00:36:39.040
you to change your analysis or your plans
for analysis.

00:36:39.040 --> 00:36:46.190
So the closer you can be involved at the start
of an experiment, the better for all of us

00:36:46.190 --> 00:36:47.430
concerned.

00:36:47.430 --> 00:36:49.520
There is of course many ways of doing that.

00:36:49.520 --> 00:36:55.330
But one of them is by working closely with
experimentalists either in a host lab or in

00:36:55.330 --> 00:36:57.320
your own lab.

00:36:57.320 --> 00:37:04.080
The other point I want to make here ends up
being actually, in my career, even more important.

00:37:04.080 --> 00:37:09.680
And it was not something that I saw immediately,
but saw over the first three or four years

00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:14.890
that I became involved in experiments alongside
data science.

00:37:14.890 --> 00:37:20.760
And that's that as you begin to get your own
hands wet or dirty or whatever the right analogy

00:37:20.760 --> 00:37:27.510
is here, this miraculous thing happens, and
you become in touch with biology like no other

00:37:27.510 --> 00:37:28.510
method.

00:37:28.510 --> 00:37:34.170
You can certainly become very educated about
biological systems as an armchair reader of

00:37:34.170 --> 00:37:38.120
those processes, either with text books or
literature or hearing about them.

00:37:38.120 --> 00:37:43.250
But somehow, when you're at the bench, you
gain a completely different level.

00:37:43.250 --> 00:37:48.640
At least in my own life it was transformative.

00:37:48.640 --> 00:37:56.200
And the real power that you then have is the
ability to see these key biological computational

00:37:56.200 --> 00:38:01.970
coupled problems that it's harder to see if
you're on just one side of that fence or the

00:38:01.970 --> 00:38:02.970
other.

00:38:02.970 --> 00:38:06.850
And it turns out [? Fisher ?] has an excellent
quote on this as well.

00:38:06.850 --> 00:38:11.840
Sanity and realism can be restored to the
teaching of mathematical statistics most directly

00:38:11.840 --> 00:38:16.880
by trusting it to men and women who have had
personal experience of research in the natural

00:38:16.880 --> 00:38:17.880
sciences.

00:38:17.880 --> 00:38:22.860
And so here, of course, he may be talking
about different ways of getting that experience,

00:38:22.860 --> 00:38:29.220
but certainly a laboratory is a wonderful
way of really getting experience beyond data

00:38:29.220 --> 00:38:33.490
sciences, which he's referring to as mathematical
statistics here.

00:38:33.490 --> 00:38:39.079
Really get involved in the biological discipline
itself.

00:38:39.079 --> 00:38:43.380
Considering this quote is circa 1930, it's
even more impressive.

00:38:43.380 --> 00:38:51.730
And then really, the third practical issue
here is once you have data in your own hands,

00:38:51.730 --> 00:38:57.060
you're much more assured that you're not duplicating
the efforts of someone else.

00:38:57.060 --> 00:39:02.740
Of course, it's certainly always feasible
that someone else has done the exact same

00:39:02.740 --> 00:39:05.200
experiment as you are.

00:39:05.200 --> 00:39:10.610
Certainly molecular biologists, geneticists,
and biochemists also get scooped.

00:39:10.610 --> 00:39:16.240
But it's a higher bar to be scooped certainly.

00:39:16.240 --> 00:39:22.040
And so, if you look at my lab, we have the
pictures you might expect, like this is [? Eric

00:39:22.040 --> 00:39:30.450
?] and [? Kay ?] in my group who are Cytoscape
team developers, looking at a network in Cytoscape.

00:39:30.450 --> 00:39:35.170
But then you have, moving from the office
to the lab here we're generating-- this is

00:39:35.170 --> 00:39:40.330
[? Brian ?] in lab generating network data
and [? Anna ?] analyzing some of the network

00:39:40.330 --> 00:39:42.900
data, for instance.

00:39:42.900 --> 00:39:48.850
And so that's been quite a fun and frustrating
and productive exercise.

00:39:48.850 --> 00:39:55.160
And to show how it has been used in our research
plan or program here, let me go back to this

00:39:55.160 --> 00:39:58.090
example of constructing hierarchical models.

00:39:58.090 --> 00:40:04.500
So I already introduced this idea that we
are trying to integrate network data of various

00:40:04.500 --> 00:40:10.640
kinds to together to build ultimately these
hierarchies or data driven gene ontologies

00:40:10.640 --> 00:40:17.770
of a particular biological process and ultimately
the whole cell as a type of whole cell model.

00:40:17.770 --> 00:40:25.030
Now, once one has some control over one's
experiments, one can shift from a mode in

00:40:25.030 --> 00:40:32.450
which one is analyzing really public interactomics
data-- that's A, B, and C-- to now asking

00:40:32.450 --> 00:40:38.290
what new data set could I generate that would
most improve this hierarchical model.

00:40:38.290 --> 00:40:43.520
That is to say, the fidelity with which this
hierarchical model represents real biology,

00:40:43.520 --> 00:40:46.020
actual biological systems.

00:40:46.020 --> 00:40:51.720
And to look at that, we of course don't know
biological truth, but we do have the very

00:40:51.720 --> 00:40:58.390
good Gene Ontology resource that's been created
from the literature over a long period of

00:40:58.390 --> 00:40:59.390
time.

00:40:59.390 --> 00:41:04.741
So if we use GO as at least a bronze standard
here-- it's not a pure gold standard, it's

00:41:04.741 --> 00:41:06.080
not perfect.

00:41:06.080 --> 00:41:12.220
But if we treat GO as some sort of bronze
standard and we ask, how will we recapitulate

00:41:12.220 --> 00:41:18.170
GO in our data-driven hierarchical model,
that's what's been shown here on the y-axis,

00:41:18.170 --> 00:41:20.880
just as a fraction of our maximal ability.

00:41:20.880 --> 00:41:28.640
What I should add here is out of the box,
this approach gets about 60% recovery of cellular

00:41:28.640 --> 00:41:34.380
component terms and their interrelationships
that are in the Gene Oncology, at least for

00:41:34.380 --> 00:41:36.690
the budding yeast [INAUDIBLE].

00:41:36.690 --> 00:41:39.310
For humans, it's a little bit lower than that.

00:41:39.310 --> 00:41:43.050
So you should be thinking perfect for us,
or the best performance we can do with about

00:41:43.050 --> 00:41:45.520
60% recovery of GO.

00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:47.790
And that's normalized here to one.

00:41:47.790 --> 00:41:56.060
And now what I'm going to do is remove studies
one-by-one from the public domain, and various

00:41:56.060 --> 00:42:00.770
kinds-- genetic interactions, protein-protein
interactions, and co-expression interactions.

00:42:00.770 --> 00:42:06.030
And after I remove a particular PubMed ID--
and that's what literally these are, is PubMed

00:42:06.030 --> 00:42:11.050
IDs-- I'm going to reevaluate my ability,
having removed that study, at recovering GO

00:42:11.050 --> 00:42:12.050
in my hierarchy.

00:42:12.050 --> 00:42:16.680
And so what you can see here for at least
these three kinds of data is that if I remove

00:42:16.680 --> 00:42:23.260
genetic interactions or protein-protein interactions,
then I do start to hurt my ability to recapitulate

00:42:23.260 --> 00:42:24.450
Go.

00:42:24.450 --> 00:42:29.530
Whereas interestingly, if I remove co-expression
studies all the way up into all 50 budding

00:42:29.530 --> 00:42:32.530
yeast co-expression studies that were in the
database we accessed.

00:42:32.530 --> 00:42:38.300
Given that you still have all of the other
data around in the model.

00:42:38.300 --> 00:42:39.540
You don't hurt your performance.

00:42:39.540 --> 00:42:45.560
And so this allowed us to choose, in this
case, genetic interactions as the strongest

00:42:45.560 --> 00:42:46.980
negative slope here.

00:42:46.980 --> 00:42:50.550
That is to say, the interactions which at
least at the present time appear to be the

00:42:50.550 --> 00:42:53.869
most important for driving this model.

00:42:53.869 --> 00:42:59.590
And so now, because we had a research lab
that over a few years, I geared towards interaction

00:42:59.590 --> 00:43:08.850
mapping-- and I should say, our lab is certainly
not the highest throughput interaction mapping

00:43:08.850 --> 00:43:09.850
labs out there.

00:43:09.850 --> 00:43:15.770
There are many labs that surpass our capacity
of our jack of all trades lab, if you like.

00:43:15.770 --> 00:43:19.180
But nonetheless, we have some ability to generate
interaction maps.

00:43:19.180 --> 00:43:23.470
And so here we took 50-- this is back from
the autophagy model.

00:43:23.470 --> 00:43:30.470
And we took autophagy genes and paired them
with about 3,000 yeast genes to look for genetic

00:43:30.470 --> 00:43:32.869
interactions in a matrix-like format.

00:43:32.869 --> 00:43:36.080
So all pairs of 50 by 3,000 are tested.

00:43:36.080 --> 00:43:38.580
And we test for interaction across three conditions.

00:43:38.580 --> 00:43:44.000
And then you can also look differentially
between-- at interaction changes between those

00:43:44.000 --> 00:43:46.110
three autophagyrelevant conditions.

00:43:46.110 --> 00:43:52.369
And what you find is, here's another way again
of looking at the similarity with Go.

00:43:52.369 --> 00:43:53.369
I apologize.

00:43:53.369 --> 00:43:55.710
We've changed here the metric.

00:43:55.710 --> 00:44:01.490
But nonetheless, here is how well you do at
recovering GO with all prior data in the public

00:44:01.490 --> 00:44:02.490
domain.

00:44:02.490 --> 00:44:06.770
If you look at just this screen alone, given
you've targeted one particular process in

00:44:06.770 --> 00:44:11.800
your lab with the right gene sets and the
right conditions, it turns out that even not

00:44:11.800 --> 00:44:17.720
considering prior data, you can do better
than all of those public domain data.

00:44:17.720 --> 00:44:22.040
And again, it's just because you've tailored
the data in terms of conditions and genes.

00:44:22.040 --> 00:44:26.040
But of course, if you add both the new data
and the prior data together, the recovery

00:44:26.040 --> 00:44:29.350
of the GO hierarchy gets even better.

00:44:29.350 --> 00:44:36.200
And over here are some controls showing that
if you randomize things, then you [? erode

00:44:36.200 --> 00:44:38.340
?] your performance as expected.

00:44:38.340 --> 00:44:46.110
So that's just the latest vignette, really,
of how we've put together a public data set

00:44:46.110 --> 00:44:51.420
analysis leading to a
collaboration with an autophagy lab with then

00:44:51.420 --> 00:44:57.300
let us generate our own network data, and
come full circle on that systems biology cycle

00:44:57.300 --> 00:44:58.300
I showed.

00:44:58.300 --> 00:45:02.500
Now, I don't want to build this for everyone
here.

00:45:02.500 --> 00:45:08.780
And I should say, there's many frustrations
with getting involved in these kinds of experiments

00:45:08.780 --> 00:45:10.990
yourself as a data scientist.

00:45:10.990 --> 00:45:17.940
It is a massive investment of your time that
could otherwise be going to data science research

00:45:17.940 --> 00:45:19.940
directly.

00:45:19.940 --> 00:45:27.110
Compounding this fact is that debugging lab
work is much slower than debugging other kinds

00:45:27.110 --> 00:45:28.170
of computation.

00:45:28.170 --> 00:45:32.730
And I think several of us have appreciated
that over the years.

00:45:32.730 --> 00:45:38.790
You can press Enter and test whether your
program works in a split second, typically.

00:45:38.790 --> 00:45:44.680
But pressing Enter in lab can take days before
you get a result.

00:45:44.680 --> 00:45:47.760
So two, it costs more.

00:45:47.760 --> 00:45:55.210
You have to not only maintain your space and
personnel for the data sciences part of your

00:45:55.210 --> 00:46:00.260
lab, or the data analysis side of your lab,
you have to come up with those resources of

00:46:00.260 --> 00:46:03.380
course for experimental side of the lab.

00:46:03.380 --> 00:46:05.560
And that is a challenge.

00:46:05.560 --> 00:46:09.930
You have to expand your focus, which means
that you're distracted.

00:46:09.930 --> 00:46:15.050
You have more to worry about, and that may
make you a less successful scientist at any

00:46:15.050 --> 00:46:17.550
particular task.

00:46:17.550 --> 00:46:21.860
This is what people call the jack of all trades.

00:46:21.860 --> 00:46:26.710
And so another way of saying this-- or another
implication of this is that specialized biologist

00:46:26.710 --> 00:46:32.770
collaborators will not always be better than
you, but have a distinctive advantage over

00:46:32.770 --> 00:46:33.770
you.

00:46:33.770 --> 00:46:40.421
You will never be as high as they are in knowledge
of a particular biological process or the

00:46:40.421 --> 00:46:45.480
particular tool box of experiments that one
uses to interrogate that process.

00:46:45.480 --> 00:46:50.960
And perhaps most frustrating of all, while
you spend the time diversifying your computer

00:46:50.960 --> 00:46:56.100
science background to learn experimental biology,
you're going to find that your colleagues

00:46:56.100 --> 00:47:02.950
who are staying strictly in computer science
domains are going to now, over time, be better

00:47:02.950 --> 00:47:05.640
at you at computer science.

00:47:05.640 --> 00:47:06.950
And that's also frustrating.

00:47:06.950 --> 00:47:09.280
But you can collaborate both ways.

00:47:09.280 --> 00:47:13.520
I find at this point in my career even though
I started with a degree in computer science

00:47:13.520 --> 00:47:19.050
and ended with a PhD in molecular biology,
I now am in a position where I collaborate

00:47:19.050 --> 00:47:21.650
freely with both sides of that fence.

00:47:21.650 --> 00:47:24.490
And I've got to.

00:47:24.490 --> 00:47:25.490
OK.

00:47:25.490 --> 00:47:29.640
So that was the bulk of what I wanted to talk
about today.

00:47:29.640 --> 00:47:36.600
I mentioned that getting data was by far the
most weighty topic in my mind.

00:47:36.600 --> 00:47:43.619
But let me just say, in the last five minutes
or so, a few words about both computing infrastructure

00:47:43.619 --> 00:47:45.640
and process.

00:47:45.640 --> 00:47:52.750
So I think about of course all of data science
activities require computing infrastructure.

00:47:52.750 --> 00:47:54.850
And so how should we think about that?

00:47:54.850 --> 00:47:57.500
I think about it in terms of at least five
tiers.

00:47:57.500 --> 00:48:00.430
And these are not meant to be mutually exclusive.

00:48:00.430 --> 00:48:07.330
So there's the connectivity and the interface,
which sounds trivial, but it requires some

00:48:07.330 --> 00:48:08.330
thought in design.

00:48:08.330 --> 00:48:11.460
You're going to have to
separate if you have a wet lab and if you

00:48:11.460 --> 00:48:14.119
separate that out from the dry lab or that's
necessarily separate.

00:48:14.119 --> 00:48:18.760
So you have to think about how you're going
to maintain connectivity from a data point

00:48:18.760 --> 00:48:19.760
of view.

00:48:19.760 --> 00:48:25.790
I [? fear ?] and I think certainly the goal
in the lab would be to provision both environments

00:48:25.790 --> 00:48:29.860
with high bandwidth connections, both wired
and Wi-Fi.

00:48:29.860 --> 00:48:37.710
You have to think about how your personal--
or personnel, I should say here-are going

00:48:37.710 --> 00:48:40.680
to be equipped with their personal machines.

00:48:40.680 --> 00:48:46.050
So again, sounds trivial but it requires someone
to think about those kinds of things which

00:48:46.050 --> 00:48:52.410
as your lab grows can be a surprisingly large
amount of someone's time.

00:48:52.410 --> 00:48:58.670
Now beyond someone's local desktop, you have
two major resources in the data sciences laboratory

00:48:58.670 --> 00:48:59.670
to think about.

00:48:59.670 --> 00:49:04.320
You have the raw CPU power or compute engines
that you'd like to be able to access and you're

00:49:04.320 --> 00:49:07.960
going to find are a necessary part of working
with big data.

00:49:07.960 --> 00:49:13.060
And of course, storage of those data are necessary--
is a necessary part of working with big data.

00:49:13.060 --> 00:49:17.600
Here we use both private servers and the cloud.

00:49:17.600 --> 00:49:25.040
The other point to make about the compute
engines is we're seeing a trend just in the

00:49:25.040 --> 00:49:34.930
past year or two towards these GPUs or Graphics
Processing Units as ready-made cards by video

00:49:34.930 --> 00:49:40.090
game-- made originally for video games and
high performance graphics, for those kinds

00:49:40.090 --> 00:49:41.090
of applications.

00:49:41.090 --> 00:49:43.560
Like companies like Nvidia.

00:49:43.560 --> 00:49:51.700
They're essentially highly processed or highly
parallel CPUs that one can avail oneself of

00:49:51.700 --> 00:49:53.720
if one has the right libraries.

00:49:53.720 --> 00:49:59.210
And it turns out that now Nvidia in particular
supports the libraries for scientific computing

00:49:59.210 --> 00:50:03.950
on GPUs, and it's really been revolutionary
to not just my lab but to a lot of labs I

00:50:03.950 --> 00:50:05.280
know in the data sciences.

00:50:05.280 --> 00:50:10.080
So if you're just getting started, really
look seriously at access to GPUs.

00:50:10.080 --> 00:50:15.940
From your own lab, they're relatively inexpensive.

00:50:15.940 --> 00:50:22.830
Now storage-wise, we do have private storage
and also cloud storage.

00:50:22.830 --> 00:50:27.870
And the private storage, there's a variety
of reasons one wishes to have private storage.

00:50:27.870 --> 00:50:34.680
Not least of which, as we begin to get data
sets for patience, one has to worry about

00:50:34.680 --> 00:50:38.109
HIPAA requirements dealing with patient privacy.

00:50:38.109 --> 00:50:43.550
And it's easier to worry about those if one
has a mobile storage unit.

00:50:43.550 --> 00:50:47.250
But of course, we're also seeing HIPAA compliance
in the cloud.

00:50:47.250 --> 00:50:54.730
So whether you go local or cloud, really starting
to become more of a personal decision than

00:50:54.730 --> 00:51:02.330
one in which it's easy to advise one way or
the other.

00:51:02.330 --> 00:51:08.430
I would say the other big consideration here
is latency.

00:51:08.430 --> 00:51:13.040
One would like to have quick access to the
data, which of course if the connection is

00:51:13.040 --> 00:51:17.050
to a machine right next to you, that latency
tends to be much less.

00:51:17.050 --> 00:51:19.570
But that's also changing.

00:51:19.570 --> 00:51:26.040
Here is what this-- all this looks like, which
I think it always helps to get some perspective.

00:51:26.040 --> 00:51:31.520
I'm not going to spend much time on this slide
other than to say, we here have a lot of the

00:51:31.520 --> 00:51:37.640
local machines I talked about that compute
and the storage here at the San Diego Supercomputing

00:51:37.640 --> 00:51:44.900
Center, which is just a half campus away,
so about a quarter mile away from my lab,

00:51:44.900 --> 00:51:49.040
with fast connectivity to both the experimental
and computational lab.

00:51:49.040 --> 00:51:51.450
This is the schematic of that space.

00:51:51.450 --> 00:51:56.970
And so over here, you see that this is the
compute part of that cluster.

00:51:56.970 --> 00:51:59.970
And here is the storage part of that cluster.

00:51:59.970 --> 00:52:02.720
And here is representation of the local machines.

00:52:02.720 --> 00:52:10.400
And so of course, the physical location changes
between the local machines and lab and the

00:52:10.400 --> 00:52:13.000
STSC machines and so on.

00:52:13.000 --> 00:52:19.540
OK, so the final thing I wanted to just touch
on is software development process.

00:52:19.540 --> 00:52:28.910
As we begin to think about our data sciences
projects, it helps greatly-- and this, I should

00:52:28.910 --> 00:52:37.210
say, took me 10 years and a key conversation
with Dr. Tamara Munzner at UBC who urged me--

00:52:37.210 --> 00:52:41.090
she runs a very successful lab at UBC in data
sciences.

00:52:41.090 --> 00:52:49.500
And a few years ago, she urged me to think
about my tool development across four stages,

00:52:49.500 --> 00:52:55.020
and whether she would accredit this to someone
else maybe she would.

00:52:55.020 --> 00:52:58.010
But I wanted to give her her credit here.

00:52:58.010 --> 00:53:05.260
So first is to identify and motivate the problem
or the driving biologic question, along with

00:53:05.260 --> 00:53:15.590
the target community that most stands to benefit
from answering that biological question or

00:53:15.590 --> 00:53:18.220
successfully solving that problem.

00:53:18.220 --> 00:53:25.910
Stage two is develop a prototype, and not
worry about yet how you're going to convince

00:53:25.910 --> 00:53:29.380
the world that your prototype is useful.

00:53:29.380 --> 00:53:31.660
You've already identified the need.

00:53:31.660 --> 00:53:38.390
Now in stage two, spend some time internal
to the lab and get out that first paper.

00:53:38.390 --> 00:53:45.480
Once that's out, then you can focus in stage
three on making a robust software tool or

00:53:45.480 --> 00:53:49.350
a website or database as the case may be.

00:53:49.350 --> 00:53:56.280
And that's not the initial point of that first
paper in stage two.

00:53:56.280 --> 00:53:57.810
That comes later.

00:53:57.810 --> 00:54:02.570
And finally, once you have a [INAUDIBLE] that
begins to take a life of its own, perhaps

00:54:02.570 --> 00:54:07.940
you even generates funding behind it, then
you can worry about broad dissemination and

00:54:07.940 --> 00:54:13.780
steps you can take to broadly encourage the
community and make your approach available

00:54:13.780 --> 00:54:15.920
and useful.

00:54:15.920 --> 00:54:21.780
So the first time we thought about this type
of four-stage process was in planning our

00:54:21.780 --> 00:54:26.850
national resource for network biology, where
Dr. Munzner is an advisor.

00:54:26.850 --> 00:54:30.990
She convinced us to take all of our projects
and to make this kind of chart.

00:54:30.990 --> 00:54:34.720
And so on the-- I'm not going to go, of course,
into any detail here, but just to show you

00:54:34.720 --> 00:54:38.590
kind of the big organizational view of all
of this.

00:54:38.590 --> 00:54:42.910
So [? and-- ?] [? in ?] rows of this chart,
you have that different technology research

00:54:42.910 --> 00:54:48.750
and development projects or TRDs, the different
groups in this consortium that are signed

00:54:48.750 --> 00:54:49.750
up for those.

00:54:49.750 --> 00:54:54.020
But then importantly, across the columns here,
you see those four stages.

00:54:54.020 --> 00:55:00.090
And so now all of our research-- well, first
of all planning in stage one, then research

00:55:00.090 --> 00:55:04.280
in stage two and three, and development in
stages three and four-it all now gets thought

00:55:04.280 --> 00:55:07.640
about in terms of a four-stage model.

00:55:07.640 --> 00:55:12.340
In terms of, for instance, our Cytoscape software,
here's how I think about the four stages.

00:55:12.340 --> 00:55:19.770
So, and this is, arguably for my lab, the
first software tool that came out of my lab

00:55:19.770 --> 00:55:24.500
in collaboration with several others, started
back in the year 2000.

00:55:24.500 --> 00:55:31.240
And so the first was to identify the driving
problem in the target community, which here,

00:55:31.240 --> 00:55:34.290
at
the time, it was clear that molecular biologists

00:55:34.290 --> 00:55:36.750
were generating ever larger molecular networks.

00:55:36.750 --> 00:55:41.010
We've already talked all about the fruits
of that, 15 years later.

00:55:41.010 --> 00:55:45.220
But at the time, there really were no tools
to analyze networks that large.

00:55:45.220 --> 00:55:51.980
There were certainly tools for Cytoscape,
but nothing of the magnitude that was needed

00:55:51.980 --> 00:55:54.850
or the analytical complexity that was needed.

00:55:54.850 --> 00:55:56.850
And so that was the motivation.

00:55:56.850 --> 00:56:04.580
We first-- decode it ended up becoming did
first was used in a series of prototype studies,

00:56:04.580 --> 00:56:09.910
which were biological studies performed and
published over a few papers between the years

00:56:09.910 --> 00:56:12.220
2000 and 2003.

00:56:12.220 --> 00:56:16.560
And the actual publication and creation of
Cytoscape itself, its initial release was

00:56:16.560 --> 00:56:18.200
in 2002.

00:56:18.200 --> 00:56:21.760
And its initial marker paper was in 2003.

00:56:21.760 --> 00:56:26.290
But again, it was that real evolution of the
code, stating all the way back to even the

00:56:26.290 --> 00:56:32.220
late '90s through 2003 when we announced that
tool to the world.

00:56:32.220 --> 00:56:36.770
Now since then, of course, we continue to
evolve the tool quite dramatically every two

00:56:36.770 --> 00:56:37.770
to three years.

00:56:37.770 --> 00:56:44.150
And so every three or so years, you want to
think about updating that effort with a publication.

00:56:44.150 --> 00:56:50.130
But now, as the last thing here, how did we
get broad adoption of this Cytoscape resource?

00:56:50.130 --> 00:56:56.640
And that really is where I think luck played
as much of a role as skill or planning.

00:56:56.640 --> 00:56:58.690
But there are definitely lessons to be learned.

00:56:58.690 --> 00:57:06.720
And the first big decision we made here was
to allow the Cytoscape core, or to define

00:57:06.720 --> 00:57:11.690
and separate out a part of Cytoscape which
was called the core, and a part which were

00:57:11.690 --> 00:57:12.890
called plug-ins at the time.

00:57:12.890 --> 00:57:19.500
Now they're called apps after the iPhone came
along.

00:57:19.500 --> 00:57:23.670
Giving people the ability to write their own
plug-ins or apps really changed everything

00:57:23.670 --> 00:57:24.670
for us.

00:57:24.670 --> 00:57:30.490
And so if you want, if you're willing again
to put in the software engineering effort,

00:57:30.490 --> 00:57:36.540
then it can pay off in terms of dissemination
and crowdsourcing because you're giving them

00:57:36.540 --> 00:57:40.050
a mechanism by which to very effectively crowdsource.

00:57:40.050 --> 00:57:46.740
Many of these apps-- and there's over 200
of them that are now developed for Cytoscape--

00:57:46.740 --> 00:57:49.140
are also themselves open source.

00:57:49.140 --> 00:57:54.740
And so what that means is we can actually
absorb some of that technology and use it

00:57:54.740 --> 00:57:59.740
to benefit the core in future releases, and
everyone's happy.

00:57:59.740 --> 00:58:03.410
And that's really been a powerful model for
us.

00:58:03.410 --> 00:58:05.880
Other things that we've done are maybe more
usual.

00:58:05.880 --> 00:58:11.830
So we have an international meeting around
the increasing core development community

00:58:11.830 --> 00:58:16.180
around-- and plug-in developer community--
for Cytoscape, as well as heavy biological

00:58:16.180 --> 00:58:18.320
users of Cytoscape were invited to this.

00:58:18.320 --> 00:58:22.800
Over the past several years, we've had a very
successful joining with the DREAM and the

00:58:22.800 --> 00:58:24.410
RECOMB Systems Biology Conference.

00:58:24.410 --> 00:58:29.220
We do Cytoscape in conjunction as a sort of
three-for-all.

00:58:29.220 --> 00:58:32.540
And that's been a great development.

00:58:32.540 --> 00:58:38.750
Of course, one also wants to foster local
community groups around your tool.

00:58:38.750 --> 00:58:42.850
And that's-- and one can identify
individuals who are willing to take lead roles

00:58:42.850 --> 00:58:43.850
in that.

00:58:43.850 --> 00:58:50.020
And finally, one has to fund these efforts
as they progress and grow.

00:58:50.020 --> 00:58:55.180
And here, I think if there's any one piece
of advice I have beyond the obvious, it's

00:58:55.180 --> 00:58:58.350
let go of ownership.

00:58:58.350 --> 00:59:02.930
Realize that you're going to be successful
if your software is successful.

00:59:02.930 --> 00:59:10.280
And you want people to write grants that are
essentially extending your software or taking

00:59:10.280 --> 00:59:12.640
ownership over your software even.

00:59:12.640 --> 00:59:19.290
And so, becoming an open source project, facilitating
a developer community, and just letting go

00:59:19.290 --> 00:59:25.859
of the reins as much as possible-- you know,
of course, one of course wants to continue

00:59:25.859 --> 00:59:34.609
to contribute to the project-- has been a
really, really key guideline for us.

00:59:34.609 --> 00:59:42.940
And so not only now do we have Cytoscape itself,
but we have this whole satellites of tools

00:59:42.940 --> 00:59:49.180
that have been built around Cytoscape, including
the app store where you can download the apps.

00:59:49.180 --> 00:59:53.960
Some of these apps get downloaded thousands
of times and cited thousands of times.

00:59:53.960 --> 00:59:58.600
So they themselves in their own right are
quite powerful forces.

00:59:58.600 --> 01:00:03.380
And I already talked about our connections
to data basis, like NDEx.

01:00:03.380 --> 01:00:05.930
So that concludes my talk.

01:00:05.930 --> 01:00:12.830
And I have tried to touch, really, on four
different points, which were key in building

01:00:12.830 --> 01:00:19.900
my own data sciences laboratory, and I think
are clearly going to be key considerations

01:00:19.900 --> 01:00:24.020
for anyone getting started or building into
the future.

01:00:24.020 --> 01:00:28.750
And so with that, I will thank you for your
time, especially given that I think I just

01:00:28.750 --> 01:00:32.240
competed with Donald Trump's inaugural address.

01:00:32.240 --> 01:00:35.790
If you are still on this call, I think you
made the right decision to stay.

01:00:35.790 --> 01:00:39.790
But with that, I will take any questions.

01:00:39.790 --> 01:00:43.030
Thank you, Trey, for this wonderful talk.

01:00:43.030 --> 01:00:46.670
So we actually have time for one quick question.

01:00:46.670 --> 01:00:50.000
So the first question-well, actually, we got
a lot of questions for this.

01:00:50.000 --> 01:00:52.990
But I'm just going to choose one.

01:00:52.990 --> 01:00:57.910
So what are your thoughts on using-- makerspaces
for data time science labs?

01:00:57.910 --> 01:00:59.390
Do you have any thoughts on that?

01:00:59.390 --> 01:01:03.940
Are there any data science makers out there
who use a space like this?

01:01:03.940 --> 01:01:08.440
That is a wonderful suggestion.

01:01:08.440 --> 01:01:14.340
I have several lab members who are big participants
in the makers movement.

01:01:14.340 --> 01:01:19.590
I, myself, have not-- have not tried going
down that road.

01:01:19.590 --> 01:01:24.670
My guess is there's-- depending on what environment
you are in.

01:01:24.670 --> 01:01:30.980
If you're in a university environment that
has a biological sciences department, my guess

01:01:30.980 --> 01:01:36.420
is it might actually be easier just to go
to your neighbor and use their maker space

01:01:36.420 --> 01:01:39.790
that's provided by the university.

01:01:39.790 --> 01:01:43.300
But I would love to hear people have had experiences
with that.

01:01:43.300 --> 01:01:44.300
OK.

01:01:44.300 --> 01:01:47.800
Well, we're out of time.

01:01:47.800 --> 01:01:50.460
Thank you so much.

01:01:50.460 --> 01:01:53.700
And we'll conclude this talk today.

01:01:53.700 --> 01:01:54.999
Thank you.

